1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:08,855
An agent that buys things is the sharpest
case of excessive agency. A payment cannot

2
00:00:08,855 --> 00:00:10,155
be undone.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:16,688
So the agent is given a mandate. A cap per
transaction, a total cap, a validity

4
00:00:16,688 --> 00:00:22,088
window, a list of merchants. And the
mandate is enforced by code before the

5
00:00:22,088 --> 00:00:23,816
call, not audited after.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:31,158
The agent reads a page and proposes six
euros forty for a ream of paper. The

7
00:00:31,158 --> 00:00:36,586
mandate allows it, PayPal creates the
order. And from that transaction reference

8
00:00:36,586 --> 00:00:41,948
alone, everything comes back: what was
read, what was decided, what the mandate

9
00:00:41,948 --> 00:00:43,712
permitted at that instant.

10
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:51,582
Now the same page, and the printer at
eighty nine ninety. The price is real; the

11
00:00:51,582 --> 00:00:56,676
agent is not hallucinating. But the
mandate allows forty per transaction.

12
00:00:56,676 --> 00:01:01,979
Watch where this stops. The gateway
refuses, and PayPal is never called. Not

13
00:01:01,979 --> 00:01:07,561
refunded, not reversed. Never called. A
cap checked after the call is not a cap,

14
00:01:07,561 --> 00:01:08,861
it is a report.

15
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:21,223
Now a hostile page. It gives no order: it
says the item is discontinued, and that

16
00:01:21,223 --> 00:01:26,511
someone else ships it now. The agent reads
that correctly and proposes twenty nine

17
00:01:26,511 --> 00:01:31,219
ninety nine to a different payee. The
grounding check passes, because the

18
00:01:31,219 --> 00:01:36,572
attacker wrote the number on the page. The
cap passes, because twenty nine is under

19
00:01:36,572 --> 00:01:39,280
forty. The merchant list is what stops it.

20
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:47,027
A refusal is written to the register
exactly like a payment, because a log that

21
00:01:47,027 --> 00:01:52,117
only shows successes describes an agent
that never tried anything forbidden. And

22
00:01:52,117 --> 00:01:56,889
if someone rewrites that file, the
verifier does not answer that the log is

23
00:01:56,889 --> 00:01:58,416
fine. It names the line.

24
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:10,708
What this is not. It does not stop a
malicious agent: it constrains the path

25
00:02:10,708 --> 00:02:15,539
that goes through it. And out-of-register
payments are not verified unless you

26
00:02:15,539 --> 00:02:20,432
supply a webhook log, because an empty
list because nothing was searched is not

27
00:02:20,432 --> 00:02:22,848
an empty list because nothing is there.

28
00:02:23,848 --> 00:02:29,070
None of this is payment plumbing. PayPal
already had idempotency keys, webhooks and

29
00:02:29,070 --> 00:02:34,041
refunds. What it did not have was the link
between the money that moved and the

30
00:02:34,041 --> 00:02:37,816
reasoning that moved it. That link is what
you just watched.

31
00:02:38,816 --> 00:02:46,705
121 tests, no runtime dependencies, Apache
two point zero. The model reads. The code

32
00:02:46,705 --> 00:02:48,005
decides.
